{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 55, :comments ["<b>After it is washed.</b> A term of being done." "<b>The plague has not changed its appearance.</b> It has not lost its luster." "<b>And the plague has not spread.</b> We have heard that if it has not changed [color] and has not spread — it is tamei; and no need to say if it has not changed and has spread. But if it changed [color] and has not spread — I do not know what is done with it. Therefore Scripture says, ״and the plague shall be shut up״ — in any case. These are the words of R. Yehudah; and the Sages say etc., as is found in Torat Kohanim. And I have alluded to it here to settle the verse on its own terms." "<b>It is a depression.</b> A term of a pit, as in ״בְּאַחַת הַפְּחָתִים״ (II Samuel 17) — meaning it is sunken: a plague whose appearance is depressed (Sifra)." "<b>In its bald spot or in its bald forehead.</b> As the Targum renders: in its baldness or in its forepart baldness." "<b>Its bald spot.</b> Thinning, worn. And because of the midrash that was needed for a gezerah shavah — from where do we know flowering in garments that is pure? קָרַחַת and גַּבַּחַת are said of a person, and קָרַחַת and גַּבַּחַת are said of garments: just as there, if it spread in its entirety it is pure, so too here, if it spread in its entirety it is pure — therefore Scripture grasped the language of קָרַחַת and גַּבַּחַת. And as for its meaning and its Targum, this is what it conveys: קָרַחַת is a term for the back and גַּבַּחַת is a term for the front — as if it were written 'in its hind part' or 'in its fore part' — for קָרַחַת is a term for the back and גַּבַּחַת is a term for the face, as it is written ״וְאִם מִפְּאַת פָּנָיו״ etc., and קָרַחַת is everything that slopes and descends from the crown backward; so it is explained in Tg. Kr."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:06:27.413971991Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}